BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:yU3UA3YZynzVbmF3tlVYk
SUMMARY:Programmable control of small non-coding RNA expression in mammalia
	n cells with synthetic RNA polymerase III transactivators.
DTSTAMP:20260704T105328Z
DTSTART:20260710T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Matthew Demelo  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis De
	fense  Department: BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology  Location: Ke
	ck Hall 102  Reliable control of gene expression via synthetic genetic cir
	cuits is essential to develop next-generation therapies\, to facilitate ef
	ficient bioindustrial processes\, and to enable advanced methods in basic 
	cell biology. Synthetic control of small non-coding RNAs (sncRNAs) is of i
	mmense interest due to their important roles in development and disease in
	 mammals\, as well as their central role in technologies such as CRISPR/Ca
	s gene editing and RNA interference. RNA polymerase III (Pol III) is the c
	anonical polymerase for sncRNA expression\, but few tools for regulated Po
	l III expression exist\, requiring expression from RNA polymerase II (Pol 
	II) promoters. While there has been extensive development of synthetic reg
	ulatory methods for expressing protein-coding RNAs and large non-coding RN
	As via Pol II\, these methods are often ineffective for robust expression 
	of functional sncRNAs and often require post-transcriptional processing fo
	r sncRNAs to achieve functionality\, impairing the feasibility of translat
	ing sncRNA technologies into medical and industrial applications. Here\, I
	 develop a toolkit capable of highly programmable and multiplexable expres
	sion of synthetic sncRNAs using Synthetic Pol III Transcription Factors (S
	yn3TFs)\, facilitating far more effective and robust expression of sncRNAs
	 in mammals than previous methods. Additionally\, I outline concise design
	 rules for the construction of minimal Pol III promoters and complementary
	 Syn3TFs\, facilitating seamless construction of orthogonal Pol III circui
	ts with customizable outputs. Finally\, I demonstrate the utility of Syn3T
	Fs in performing complex genetic logic and acting as efficient sensors for
	 continuous molecular recording of cellular events. This platform represen
	ts a crucial first step to expanding the arsenal of circuit construction s
	trategies for sncRNA expression and provides a strong foundation for conti
	nued development of Pol III-specific synthetic regulators.  &#160\;  (Depa
	rtment : BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology)\n\nhttps://events.rice
	.edu/live/events/432246-programmable-control-of-small-non-coding-rna\n\nFr
	om Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/432246-programmable-control-of-smal
	l-non-coding-rna
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keck Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:X7_eyEfLqRtK1WCMwyvZ4
SUMMARY:Programmable control of small non-coding RNA expression in mammalia
	n cells with synthetic RNA polymerase III transactivators.
DTSTAMP:20260704T105328Z
DTSTART:20260710T200000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Matt Demelo  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defen
	se  Department: BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell Biology  Location: Keck 
	Hall 102  Reliable control of gene expression via synthetic genetic circui
	ts is essential to develop next-generation therapies\, to facilitate effic
	ient bioindustrial processes\, and to enable advanced methods in basic cel
	l biology. Synthetic control of small non-coding RNAs (sncRNAs) is of imme
	nse interest due to their important roles in development and disease in ma
	mmals\, as well as their central role in technologies such as CRISPR/Cas g
	ene editing and RNA interference. RNA polymerase III (RNAP3) is the canoni
	cal polymerase for sncRNA expression\, but few tools for regulated RNAP3 e
	xpression exist\, so genetic circuits requiring regulated sncRNA expressio
	n must use RNA polymerase II (RNAP2) promoters. While there has been exten
	sive development of synthetic regulatory methods for expressing protein-co
	ding RNAs and large non-coding RNAs via RNAP2\, these methods are often in
	effective for robust expression of functional sncRNAs and often require po
	st-transcriptional processing for sncRNAs to achieve functionality\, impai
	ring the feasibility of translating sncRNA technologies into medical and i
	ndustrial applications. Here\, we develop a toolkit capable of highly prog
	rammable and multiplexable expression of synthetic sncRNAs using Synthetic
	 RNAP3 Transcription Factors (Syn3TFs)\, achieving greater than 10-fold im
	provement in efficiency compared to analogous RNAP2-mediated methods. Addi
	tionally\, we outline concise design rules for the construction of minimal
	 RNAP3 promoters and complementary Syn3TFs\, facilitating seamless constru
	ction of orthogonal RNAP3 circuits with customizable outputs. Finally\, we
	 demonstrate the utility of Syn3TFs in performing complex genetic logic an
	d acting as efficient sensors for continuous molecular recording of cellul
	ar events. This platform represents a crucial first step to expanding the 
	arsenal of circuit construction strategies for sncRNA expression and provi
	des a strong foundation for continued development of RNAP3-specific synthe
	tic regulators.  &#160\;  (Department : BioSciences-Biochemistry and Cell 
	Biology)\n\nhttps://events.rice.edu/live/events/433340-programmable-contro
	l-of-small-non-coding-rna\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/433340-programmable-control-of-smal
	l-non-coding-rna
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Keck Hall
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
